<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Léman (department)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1798–1814</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Léman was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of the French Republic and Empire created in 1798, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> annexed the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a>. Its capital was Geneva, and it took its name from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-geneva">Lake Geneva</a>, Lac Léman in French. It was dissolved after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s fall, when Geneva regained its independence and joined the Swiss Confederation in 1815.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leman-department.jpg" alt="Léman (department)" width="320" height="249" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Excommunicato (original version by Naturals) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:L%C3%A9man_departement_(1812).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
